\chapter{MEMGraph and proof-directed bounds lowering}
\label{ch:77}
\index{MEMGraph and proof-directed bounds lowering}
\index{Optimization!bounds checks}

MEMGraph uses a build-time LLSynth rule profile to recognize checked fixed-array accesses with provable safe bounds. A decision can remove a runtime bounds check only when that operation cannot trap under the admitted proof. Unproved accesses retain the original checks. The optimization applies to direct C11 and the supported VM product lowering, with no application runtime dependency on the generator.

\section{Inspect a proof decision}
Write one access at a statically valid index and one at a dynamic index. Use an enabled build and inspect \OnxCodeTerm{--dump-memgraph}, then compare emitted code. Run the dynamic access for a valid index and an invalid one to show the latter still traps. Test a slicing boundary independently from indexing because legal ranges differ. Provenance of the array extent and access shape is part of the proof. A proof that one index is valid does not authorize a new ownership, foreign, or general MIR representation.

\section{Prove two endpoints, then change the receiver}
Take the fixture's array \OnxCodeTerm{array[11,22,33]}. Its extent is
three. For \OnxCodeTerm{a[2]}, the obligation is $0\leq 2<3$, so the
access yields 33. For \OnxCodeTerm{a[3:3]}, the two endpoints satisfy
$0\leq 3\leq 3\leq 3$. This is an empty slice at the end, where an
index of three would already be outside the array. Reusing the index
predicate for the upper slice endpoint would keep a check unnecessarily;
reusing the slice endpoint rule for indexing would omit a necessary check.

Next trace \OnxCodeTerm{a[1:3][1]}. Forming the slice takes elements 22
and 33. The outer access returns 33, but its receiver is now a slice,
not the original fixed array. The fixture expects the slice construction
check to disappear and the subsequent index check to remain. Knowing the
answer to this particular example is stronger information than the
implemented fixed-array rule is permitted to use.

One more pair separates bounds from evaluation. A call constructing a
three-element array may print a marker before returning it. Index that
result with literal one: the extent proof can remove the index check, but
the constructor still runs. Replace the literal with a marking function
that returns two: the base marker must appear before the index marker,
and the runtime index check remains. Counting emitted bounds calls and
recording the two markers tests both what the optimization removed and
what it had to preserve.

